# FormBuilder:  Working with Permissions

## About

Setting permissions appropriately is a necessary element of working with both FormBuilder and SearchKit.  This section uses examples to provide details of how you can do just that.  

## Combined SearchKit & FormBuilder Example

[FormBuilder - Permissions](../img/the-user-interface/form-builder/fb-sk-permissions-01.png)

In this combined SearchKit and FormBuilder example, you have a list of donations you want to expose publicly. You are aware that the role **Anonymous** has a very limited number of permissions.

You would set 
 * The SearchKit permission to **bypass permissions** so that the anonymous user can view the results.
 * The form settings permission to **Generic: Allow all users (including Anonymous)**. The **Civicontribute** default permissions will then be bypassed.

If you had set a link on a field in the search results (the contribution id, the contact, …) to see details of a contribution, a contact, specifically, the anonymous user, will not be able to access oher screens (the details). Access is being restricted to the form.

## Submission forms

There are also permissions for each entity tab (**individual1**, **activity1**, **case1**, …).  You get these results, depending on your choice:
 * **User-based**: Each entity inherits permissions based on the current user permissions (as from the CMS and ACL permissions).
 * **Form-based**: Allow access to any fields in the form.

For example, you may want to give an anonymous user the capability of creating a claim using CiviCase. The anonymous user likely does not have the permission to create a case. By setting **Form-based** permission, the user will be allowed to do so on this page.

Additional permissions for each entity are:
 * The capabliity to **create** or **update** the entity (or to do nothing). 
 * **Accept Id from Url**. If this choice is not accepted, security is generally improved. Usability may at times require this option, so be cautious in what you expose.  

 ![FormBuilder Example-Donations](../img/the-user-interface/form-builder/fb-sk-permissions-02.png)

## Additional Information on the Search forms

In the search tab, there is a possibility to expose filters via an **Id in Url** to filter the results of a SearchKit query.

![FormBuilder Example-Test Contribution](../img/the-user-interface/form-builder/fb-sk-permissions-03.png)

The URL query will be in the form of https://yoursite.org/civicrm/your-page-route/#?id=11. The **Page Route** is set on the form settings page.

When setting this filter, ids will be displayed. Make sure permissions are consistent.

### Security Check

A security issue to check is the ability to change the id manually. As soon as an id in the Url exists, ACL permissions have to be set precisely.

### Submission forms Use cases

Use Case 1: you want to give a logged-in user the possibility to update his personal data, without giving him the possibility to update other contact data.

The user has been given ACL permissions:access to dashboard, edit contact

Form settings: 
 * Permission: Access to contact dashboard so that the user is able to open the form.
 * Expose: message token (to get the token for the mailing).
 * Create a page route.
 * Accessible on front end of website.

Individual Entity settings
 * Update and Do not accept id in URL. 
 * Autofill: current user so that the fields are populated.
 * Security: User based.

You send the mail with the token.  
Individual fields are prefilled, and can be updated.  
The user can create the activity and set activity fields.  

Use Case 2: you want to send a questionnaire to an individual who does not have an account. He will be able to update some contact fields, an activity is created and he will be able to set some fields in the activity.

The anonymous user has been given ACL permissions: **Profile view**, **edit contact**.

Form settings: 
 * Permission: Profile view so that the user is able to open the form.
 * Expose: message token (to get the token for the mailing).
 * Create a page route.
 * Accessible on front end of website.

Individual Entity settings
 * Update and Do not accept id in URL. 
 * Autofill: current user so that the fields will be populated.
 * Security: in that case it is advised to set **Form-based** rather than **User-based**.

Activity Entity settings: 
 * **Create** only
 * Security: **Form-based**

You send the mail with the token.  
Individual fields are prefilled, and can be updated.  
The user can create the activity and set activity fields.  

Use Case 3:  you want to give to a logged in user the list of donations of the month. However, you do not want to give the permission **CiviContribute: access Contribute** to the role this user has been assigned to.

User Role permission: **CiviCRM: access Contact Dashboard** is a standard logged in permission.

SearchKit permissions: Bypass permissions so that contributions are visible.

Form settings: 
 * Permission: **CiviCRM: access Contact Dashboard** (anonymous user will NOT be able to see this form).
 * Create a page route.
 * Accessible on front end of website.  

Search tab settings
 * No filter

There is space here for more use cases.  Contact the **Document Working Group** (link to be provided) if you wish to contribute to this list (or, in general, if you have comments about this material).  

### Limitations

Here is an example.

    You create a form for anonymous users to update their contact data.  
    You set **Edit my contact** in CMS permissions for Anonymous users.  
    In the form you define the following settings:  
    In the Form settings tab, **Form permission: Edit my contact**.  
    You expose a token.  
    In the individual tab, Autofill “Current user”, User-based, ID in URL NOT ticked.  
    You send the form with a token.  
    The form is displayed to the anonymous user. Updates are submitted but actually not saved.  
    It should have worked, but, currently, due to API limitations, it does not.

The Form based approach should be taken as a workaround.

